<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gutians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gutian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gutians were a people from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a> who raided and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in the late third millennium BCE. Their incursions contributed to the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>, after which they held power in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>. Their expulsion was followed by the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-sumerian-empire">Neo-Sumerian Empire</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Third Dynasty of Ur</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gutians.jpg" alt="Gutians" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 0x010C · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tablet_of_Lugalannatum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Guti were a people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>, and their homeland was known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutium">Gutium</a>. Little is known of their origins, material culture or language, since contemporary sources give few details and no artifacts have been positively identified. The names of their kings suggest a language not closely related to Sumerian, Akkadian, Hurrian, Hattic, Eblaite, Amorite or Elamite. Boris Piotrovsky and Grigory Bongard-Levin suggested it may have been cognate with Hurrian. Most scholars have not accepted attempts to link the royal names to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European languages</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Gutians appear in a later copy of an inscription of Lugal-Anne-Mundu of Adab, among the nations giving him tribute. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/year-name">Year-names</a> of Shar-kali-sharri of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> record that in one year he captured Sharlag, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutium">Gutium</a>, and in another that &quot;the yoke was imposed on Gutium&quot;. The later composition Great Revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> has Naram-Sin of Akkad defeating the Gutian king Gula'an, although 90,000 of his soldiers were slain.</p><p>During the Akkadian period the Gutians grew in strength and established a capital at Adab. They eventually overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a> says their army subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruk">Uruk</a> for hegemony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a>, in about 2147–2050 BCE. Autonomous rulers soon arose again in several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> appear not to have been overrun. On a statue at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nippur">Nippur</a> the Gutian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erridupizir">Erridupizir</a> styles himself &quot;King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutium">Gutium</a>, King of the Four Quarters&quot;. The King List credits <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu-hengal">Utu-hengal</a> of Uruk with defeating the Gutian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirigan">Tirigan</a> and removing the Guti from the country in about 2050 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Later writers portrayed the Guti as uncouth. The Weidner Chronicle, written about 500 BCE, calls them unhappy people who did not know how to revere the gods, and the Victory Stele of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu-hengal">Utu-hengal</a> describes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutium">Gutium</a> as a fanged snake of the mountains. By the middle of the 2nd millennium BCE the name had been extended to all foreigners from north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros Mountains</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, so that Assyrian annals call <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrians">Hurrians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a> or Mannaeans Gutians. As late as the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> the general Gubaru was described as &quot;governor of Gutium&quot;, after which the name disappears.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gutians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The military threat came from the <b>Gutians</b>, a highland people from the Zagros mountains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Akkadian Empire fell in the 22nd century BCE to internal strain and to the <b>Gutians</b> from the Zagros Mountains, and it is not known whether a Gutian king or a local governor then ruled Ur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ur">Ur · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Utu-hengal expelled the <b>Gutians</b>, power passed almost at once to Ur-Nammu of Ur, probably his brother or a governor he had appointed, who founded the Third Dynasty of Ur; Uruk was honoured as a cult centre but governed from Ur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uruk">Uruk · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Gutians</b> came from the central Zagros and raided the plains as the Akkadian Empire weakened.</blockquote><a href="/state/gutian-rule">Gutian rule</a></li></ul></section>
